  [1mNext fork: how [38;5;153msettings.js[39m gets into the page.[0m [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is a classic global script, so the new module needs a loading
  mechanism, and this one sets the interface every future consumer uses.

  - [1mSecond [38;5;153m<script>[39m tag, global object[0m — add [38;5;153m<script src="settings.js">[39m before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m; settings.js sets [38;5;153mwindow.SETTINGS
[39m    [38;5;153m= {...}[39m and [38;5;153mapp.js[39m reads it. Matches the file's existing style exactly, and [38;5;153mindex.html[39m keeps working when opened
    straight off disk ([38;5;153mfile://[39m).
  - [1mES modules[0m — [38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js">[39m and [38;5;153mimport { API_ENDPOINT } from './settings.js'[39m. Real module
    boundary, explicit dependency, no globals. But [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m is fetched under CORS rules, so [1m[38;5;153mfile://[39m stops working[0m —
    you'd need to serve the page over HTTP from then on. It also makes the top-level script defer, which is harmless
    here.

  [1mMy recommendation: the global script tag.[0m It matches what's already in the file, and it doesn't quietly take away the
  ability to open [38;5;153mindex.html[39m by double-clicking it. The ES-module version is the better long-term shape, but it's a
  change to how you run the app, not just how config is stored — worth doing deliberately rather than as a side effect
  of moving one constant.
